G3. Cycles: connect refuses an edge that closes a loop
    (DATA_ACTION_ERR_INVALID; the peers list can leave such ports out).
    A deliberate feedback edge (1 cycle late) can come later.
    Also refused: a MIDI edge past MIDI_BUF_MERGE_MAX (16) sources into
    one input - midi_buf_merge would count the extra sources as drops.

G4. Process plan. After any graph change [main-thread] builds an
    immutable plan: nodes in topological order (Kahn), per input port its
    source buffers. Handed over by atomic pointer swap; [audio-thread]
    stores the plan generation it runs at cycle start (ack), [main-thread]
    frees the old plan - and buffers of removed ports - only once the ack
    passed it. The old plan can still sum a removed node's output, so
    stopping the owner is not enough.

G5. Buffers and summing, done by the plan before each node runs.
    Audio: each output port owns a max_buffer_size buffer. Input with no
    source -> shared silence, one source -> that buffer (zero copy,
    read-only to the consumer - LV2 connects it directly, CLAP copies),
    2+ -> summed into the input's own buffer.
    MIDI: one source -> that MIDI_BUF, 2+ -> the input's own MIDI_BUF,
    cleared for nframes, then midi_buf_merge.
    A node that did not write its outputs this cycle (stopped, sleeping,
    error) gets them zeroed / emptied by the plan - else downstream sums
    stale data every cycle (covers the CLAP sleeping branch too).
    Buffers are sized by the max buffer size - G8 reallocates.

G6. MIDI ports on the graph: the context-owned MIDI_BUFs from M2
