L1d. The walk, on [main-thread] at plan build, over the graph - no JACK
     calls. Nodes in the plan's order. Capture: node in = range over its
     CONNECTED inputs, audio AND midi (an instrument's path starts at its
     note port); outputs = in + L (+ L2 edge delays). Playback mirrored.
     UNCONNECTED PORTS do not join the min/max; none connected -> [0,0].
     pipewire-jack's default combine counts every port, an unconnected one
     as 0: audio in 256, an unconnected midi in, L = 64 -> output [64,320]
     instead of [320,320], a false "ambiguous". Same with an unconnected R
     channel or sidechain.
     Kept in the plan: the arrival per port (feeds L2 and L3) and, per
     exposed out, the internal path range from each exposed in plus the
     range of paths with no exposed in (playback mirrored) - all the
     callback needs.

L1e. JACK latency callback, exposed ports only. jack_set_latency_callback
     in the backend init, before activate. Per exposed port: the foreign
     peers' ranges (jack_port_get_connections - get_all_connections is not
     allowed in callbacks) + the L1d path ranges -> combine ->
     jack_port_set_latency_range. Nothing else - SKIPPED CYCLE. The table
     is handed over by atomic swap, or a mutex [main-thread] holds only
     briefly and never across a JACK call.
     LOOP: master_out -> other program -> master_in (G10 external insert)
     puts our own output into the foreign range - it grows every round.
     Cap it and log, never loop forever.
     Recompute: a plan build that changes the table calls
     jack_recompute_total_latencies() on [main-thread]. Never from the
     latency or the process callback.

L1f. Optional: read-only latency on CLAP/LV2 plugin rows - a getter per
     context, app_data adapts it.

     L1 DONE WHEN: a plugin with known latency (e.g. an LSP limiter with
     lookahead - CLAP and LV2) on the way to master shows up on master_out
     (jack_lsp -l from jack-example-tools, not installed - or log
     jack_port_get_latency_range on [main-thread]); a lookahead change
     updates it after the restart; removing the plugin removes it.

    LEVEL 2 - compensate, a delay per graph edge

     Scope: only the latency the app adds; foreign capture/playback
     latency counts as 0, so a synth is never delayed to match the sound
     card input. Global toggle, off by default.
     Every edge is app-owned, so a split that meets again (sampler ->
     master + sampler -> limiter -> master) is just two edges with
     different delays - what output delays on JACK ports could not do.
     MIDI edges are not delayed. A MIDI-driven instrument that has to line
     up with other audio gets the delay on its audio edges downstream.
     Delaying a MIDI edge (events held into later cycles with shifted
     frames) is needed only when MIDI streams with different latency merge
     into one input, or MIDI goes out to external gear that has to line up
     - later, own step, if a real need appears.
     Sequenced notes are better sent EARLY than delayed: an external
     sequencer that reads playback latency (Ardour) already does it once
     L1 reports our MIDI inputs; an internal sequencer later can use the
     L1d playback values as per-instrument look-ahead.

L2a. Solve, in the plan build after L1d: per node T = max arrival over
     its incoming AUDIO edges, across all its input ports (L/R and
     sidechains align per node, not per port); each such edge gets delay
     T - arrival. Node output arrival = max(T, MIDI arrivals) + L. Exposed
     inputs arrive at 0. A delay over COMP_MAX (e.g. 1 << 15 frames) is
     clamped and logged via context_sub_send_msg.

L2b. Delay lines per edge, owned by the plan, allocated at plan build on
     [main-thread], only for delays > 0. G5's summing reads the source
     through the edge's line - a single source input with a delay loses
     its zero copy.
     An edge whose delay did not change keeps its line (and the audio in
     it) across plan swaps; a changed one starts cleared (a click is fine
     for v1). Lines are fed every cycle, G5's zeroing included, so delayed
     tails drain and nothing stale waits for a restarted node.

L2c. The L1d walk counts the edge delays, so the exposed ports report the
     compensated latency.

L2d. Toggle: TRK_PARAM_PDC with the transport params (2T). Off -> every
     delay = 0, plan rebuilt.

     L2 DONE WHEN: an impulse through sampler -> master_in and through
     sampler -> limiter -> master_in lands on the same sample at
     master_out (two different sources likewise); master_out reports the
     longest path; toggle off restores the old behaviour.

L3. clap_audio_buffer_t.latency - the "nothing is done with the latency
    property" TODOs in clap_prepare_input_ports /
    clap_prepare_output_ports. Host -> plugin: latency from/to the audio
    interface for that port. Not the latency extension. Fill from the L1d
    per port results (capture for inputs, playback for outputs) + the
    exposed ports' foreign ranges, cached for [audio-thread] - no jack
    calls on rt.

2a. CLAP GUI GROUNDWORK (Linux). Plugins borrow the host's [main-thread] loop.
   The CLI scanf/fgets prompts block it and would freeze plugin GUIs.

2b. posix-fd-support: register/modify/unregister_fd fill a per plugin fd
    table; clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages polls it (timeout 0) and calls on_fd.
    on_fd may unregister - mark dead, sweep after. Drop fds on destroy.

2c. timer-support: register/unregister_timer fill a per plugin {id, period,
    next_due} table (CLOCK_MONOTONIC); due timers get on_timer in
    clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages. Resolution = the UI's tick.

2d. Optional wake fd: ui_layer_wake_fd() (-1 = none) - an epoll fd holding
    the plugin fds, a timerfd per timer and a periodic rt->ui timerfd.
    Readable -> call ui_layer_update_cycle, never read it. A UI may ignore it
    and just tick.

3a. PRESET SAVING for LV2 and CLAP plugins - save one plugin's current
    state as a preset of that plugin, loadable through the preset list.
    Not step 3: a preset is one plugin's sound, reused across songs; step 3
    is the song file.
    LV2: lilv_state_new_from_instance + lilv_state_save into a ~/.lv2
    bundle, then load that bundle into the world and re-read the plugin's
    preset list so it shows at once. Also the only way to get presets for
    plugins that expose none over LV2 (Pianoteq keeps its own .fxp files).
    CLAP: no standard "save into the plugin's preset folder" - the host
    saves the state extension's blob to its own preset file and lists it
    alongside the plugin's presets (e.g. a user category), loads it back
    through the state extension.

3. SAVING (the song file). Deliberately after (1) and (2): do not design
   the save format until what it must save has stopped moving - presets
   change what a plugin's restorable state even is, and the cleanup may
   change the param/state surface.
   NEVER serialise a params.c uid or a ContextId: the uid counter restarts
   at 0 every launch, so uid 7 is a different param next run. Save DOMAIN
   identity - plugin URI, the plugin's own param id (owner_id), sample path,
   port name. Every owner now supplies a real owner_id;
   After 2G and 2T it also saves: routing as graph edges, each end = (the
   owner's domain identity, port name) - never a graph port key (session
   only, like uids); transport settings (tempo, time signature, sync
   mode, the 2L compensation toggle).

4. CX_MOVED + reparenting. DEFERRED - no trigger yet.
   Only buys a non-destructive rename/move IN PLACE, which needs user-
   authored categories. 
   If built: detection is nearly free from app_intrf_cx_resync's existing
   diff ("in both, different parent"); CxEvent needs no new field (a move
   carries the OLD parent/index, like CX_REMOVED); the hashtable entry does
   not change (keyed by data_id, which is what did not change); the apply
   order INVERTS to ADD -> MOVE -> REMOVE (additions first so a move's
   destination exists, removals last so survivors are moved out of doomed
   subtrees) - getting that backwards silently frees live nodes. ui_layer
   cost is ~3 lines: mark the view touched, no pruning.
